          %h2#getting_started Getting Started
          %h3#how_to_edit_the_nlab How to edit the nLab
          %p
            Hit “edit page” to see how pages are coded. Use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sandbox"} Sandbox
            to warm up. The key point is that links to other pages are placed in [[double brackets]].
          %p There is no feature to preview your edits. Instead, submit them and then edit again. Two successive submissions with the same signature and made within 30 minutes of each other count as one.
          %h3#newpage How to start a new page
          %p
            To create a new page on the
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p In detail:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong Create a link request.
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    Identify any existing
                    = succeed "Lab" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %p
                    (At least one such page ought to exist, if your new page is related to anything, but if you just cannot think of any, use the
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sandbox"} Sandbox
                    page).
                %li
                  %p Hit “edit” on that existing page.
                %li
                  %p In the resulting edit pane, add the string
                  %p [[your new page name]]
                  %p at some sensible point in the source code.
                  %p (By default: in the “Related entries”-section.)
                %li
                  %p Hit “submit” below the edit pane.
                %li
                  %p See the existing page render again, now with your edit included. If your new page name did not exist yet as a page, you will see that string appear, in the existing entry, in gray with a clickable question mark behind it.
            %li
              %p
                %strong Satisfy the link request.
              %ol
                %li
                  %p Click on that question mark to open the edit pane for the new page.
                %li
                  %p Edit.
                %li
                  %p Once you hit “Submit” below that edit pane, the new page will appear.
              %p There is no preview mechanism: Just keep cycling through “Submit” and “Edit” until you are satisfied.
          %p
            %em> Watch out
            \: the name of a page is case sensitive, so make your link lowercase if it comes at the beginning of a sentence. ([[like this|Like this]].) We loosely agreed to try to follow that and some other naming conventions; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#naming"} below
          %p Use redirects to allow for other casing to be used when linking to the page elsewhere in the nLab.
          %h3#signing_an_edit Signing an edit
          %p
            When you edit a page, you should put your name (with normal capitalisation and spacing) in the box after ‘Submit as’. If you don’t, then your contribution will be credited to ‘Anonymous’ (formerly the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AnonymousCoward"} AnonymousCoward
          %p
            Once you edit a page for the first time, your name will appear at the bottom, grayed out with a question mark since there is no page with your name yet. You may take this as an invitation to create a user page and tell us about yourself. But if you don’t want to or don’t have the time right now, you can forget about this. If you just want to show up on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.ncatlab.org/nlab/list/people"} category: people
          %p
            To create your user page, simply click the question mark that appears next to your name at the bottom of the page after making a modification and add content to the edit box that appears. If you’d like to make a user page prior to modifying an existing page, you can do so by making some trivial modification to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sandbox"} Sandbox
          %h3#naming Naming conventions
          %p
            The following naming conventions for new entries are not set in stone, but we're following them for now. Changing page titles results in unnecessary work for the
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                lab elves
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/lab+elves"} ?
          %ul
            %li
              %p Page titles should contain only ASCII characters.
              %ul
                %li
                  Examples:
                  %code [[omega-category]]
                  instead of
                  %code [[$\omega$-category]]
                  or
                  = succeed "." do
                    %code [[∞-category]]
                %li#tricks
                  Tricks: To produce ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-category"}> category
                  ’, try
                  = succeed "." do
                    %code $\omega$-[[omega-category|category]]
                  = succeed "\u2019," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-category"} ∞-category
                  = succeed "." do
                    %code [[omega-category|∞-category]]
                  %code [[omega-category|$\omega$-category]]
                  does not work. You can also use
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    redirects
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/redirects"} ?
                  to make
                  %code [[∞-category]]
                  produce ‘
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"}> ∞-category
                  ’, but only when the page already exists.
                %li Reason: It’s possible to put non-ASCII characters in a name, but not ones generated by iTeX or &amp;…; character entities, so this can be difficult, depending on your browser. To keep things easy, therefore, use only ASCII characters in links. This also helps with creating automatic redirects (which are found by removing accents on letters).
                %li
                  Exception: We tend to use common European letters (such as ‘ö’, more or less the characters in Latin 1) in names, especially of people (such as
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} Kurt Gödel
            %li
              %p Page titles should be singular nouns.
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Examples: Use
                    %code [[category]]
                    instead of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %code [[categories]]
                    %code [[faithful functor]]
                    instead of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %code [[faithful]]
                    %code [[categorification]]
                    instead of
                    %code [[categorify]]
                    or
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[categorified]]
                %li
                  %p
                    Tricks: To produce ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"}> categories
                    ’, try
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[category|categories]]
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
                    = succeed "\u2019," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[faithful functor|faithful]] [[endofunctor]]
                    %code [[categories]]
                    produce ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"}> categories
                    ’, if the page already exists.
                %li
                  %p Reason: We want only one page on a given concept, regardless of variations in grammar.
                %li
                  %p
                    Exception: Recently we have started to have page titles that are complete sentences (without capitalization or punctuation) that state a simple theorem (typically a universal affirmation in the Aristotelian sense) such as
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+rings+are+profinite"} compact Hausdorff rings are profinite
                    or
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivations+of+smooth+functions+are+vector+fields"} derivations of smooth functions are vector fields
            %li#PageTitlesShouldBeUncapitalized
              %p Page titles should be uncapitalized, except for words that are always capitalized.
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Examples: Use
                    %code [[homotopy theory]]
                    instead of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %code [[Homotopy Theory]]
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[Lie algebra]]
                %li
                  %p
                    Tricks: To produce ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} Homotopy theory
                    is important!’, try
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[homotopy theory|Homotopy theory]] is important!
                %li
                  %p Reason: We want only one page on a given concept, regardless of variations in grammar.
            %li
              %p Except as contradicted above, use standard American English spelling conventions.
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Examples: Use
                    %code [[center]]
                    instead of
                    %code [[centre]]
                    and hyphens as shown in the ASCII-only requirement.
                %li
                  %p
                    Tricks: To produce ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"}> centre
                    ’, try
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[center|centre]]
                %li
                  %p
                    Reason: Much of this is written by
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
            %li
              %p Regardless of the above, pages named after specific categories should use the capitalised singular abbreviated form.
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Examples: Use
                    %code [[Set]]
                    instead
                    %code [[Sets]]
                    and
                    %code [[Cat]]
                    instead of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code [[Category]]
                %li
                  %p
                    Tricks: Although things like ‘
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Disc
                          %mo :
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Cat
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Disc: \Set \to \Cat
                    ’ work best in math mode and even ‘
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
                    ’ alone looks most consistent that way (with proper math-mode formatting), you have to make the link ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"}> Set
                    ’ outside of math mode.
          %h3#merging How to merge pages
          %p
            It happens at times that you may wish the material in existing pages
            %code A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %code B
          %p
            In most cases it will be best to raise the issue on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/"} nForum
          %p If you do decide to merge on your own, here are instructions.
          %blockquote
            %p
              The basic idea is evident: Merge the content, delete one of the entries and make its previous name redirect to the remaining page. But the redirecting takes some care; and a complication is that
              = succeed "Lab" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Suppose that
            %code A
            is the name of the page to be kept and and that
            %code B
            is the name of the one to be deleted. Then:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Edit
                %code A
              %ol
                %li
                  %p so that it looks like the intended merged entry.
                %li
                  %p Add the line “[[!redirects B]]” to the bottom.
                  %p
                    And in case the page
                    %code B
                    itself has [[!redirects X]]-lines in its source, copy them all over to
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code A
                %li
                  %p Submit.
            %li
              %p
                Edit
                %code B
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    by first clearing its content (e.g. with a keystroke such as
                    %code Ctrl-A
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %code Del
                %li
                  %p Click the checkbox ‘Change page name.’, which will bring up a new field ‘New name:’.
                %li
                  %p
                    In that field, append “
                    %code> &gt; history
                    ” to the string “
                    %code> B
                    ”, so that the new page name becomes “
                    %code> B &gt; history
                    ”.
                  %blockquote
                    %p (The idea is to change to any name that is neither coincides with an existing nor with a future entry.)
                  %p
                    Do
                    %em not
                    submit the edit
                    = succeed "." do
                      %em yet
                %li
                  %p Before finishing we need to work around what the wiki software thinks is intended behaviour:
                  %ol
                    %li
                      %p Click in the big edit box – this will automatically add the line “[[!redirects B]]”.
                      %blockquote
                        %p (The wiki software is trying to be helpful, not knowing that we want to delete the entry for good.)
                    %li
                      %p
                        Delete that line (to ensure that all requests for
                        %code B
                        will now take the reader to
                        = succeed ")," do
                          %code A
                    %li
                      %p
                        and instead add “
                        %code see
                        [[A]]”.
                      %blockquote
                        %p
                          (This just in case anyone ever comes across this page in the future – which should not actually happen, due to the [[!redirects B]]-command meanwhile added to
                          = succeed ".)" do
                            %code A
                %li
                  %p
                    Finally ready to submit this quasi-deletion of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %code B
                  %p But since the page’s name was changed in the process, the software will require announcing this edit in the box below the edit box. So enter there something like:
                  %p
                    “
                    %code> As we discussed, I am orphaning this page and have merged its previous content into page "A".
                    ”
                %li
                  %p Submit.
          %h3#how_to_remove_a_page How to remove a page
          %p
            Please make a request at the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/"} nForum
          %h3#how_to_contribute_to_the_nlab_in_ways_other_than_writing_articles How to contribute to the nLab in ways other than writing articles?
          %p There are numerous ways you can help out and get acquainted with the community even if you don’t (yet) feel comfortable writing math publicly on a wiki.
          %ol
            %li
              %p You can correct typos and spelling errors, fix links, create links, create redirects (there are many pages that still need redirects created for alternate and plural forms of the title), organize pages, and so on.
            %li
              %p You can ask questions (see previous question), and join discussions, at the nForum, and comment at the nCafe.
            %li
              %p You can try writing small additions to existing pages, for instance a paragraph or two that helps explain something that puzzled you initially. If you aren’t entirely positive that what you wrote was correct, when you announce your edit at Latest Changes on the nForum, you can mention that you’re unsure and someone will usually reassure you or give a correction.
          %h3#Citing How do I cite a page on the nLab?
          %p
            You should, of course, include a link or URL to the page or pages that you wish to cite. You should also give the information about which
            %em version
            of the page you are citing, since pages change over time. The version number can be found as follows: look at the bottom of the page where it says “Back in time (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            revisions)”; the current version number is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N+1
          %p You can link directly to the version you want to cite by using a url such as
          %pre
            %code http://ncatlab.org/nlab/revision/PageName/VERSIONNUMBER
          %p
            We recommend that if you only include one URL, it be of the form
            %code show/PageName
            which will point to whatever version of the page is current when it is accessed. This is because pages generally improve over time, and whoever is following your reference ought to be taken to the best, up-to-date version of the page. Anyone who cares about finding the exact version of the page that you cited can figure out how to find it in the history.
          %p
            On the other hand, if you can give two URLs (this would be cumbersome in a printed paper, but is possible with links on a web page), then it may be helpful to give the appropriate
            %code revision
            link as well as the
            %code show
            one.
          %p
            %em All nLab pages now include a “Cite” link at the bottom of the page which produces a BibTeX entry in accord with the suggestions above.
          %h2#HowToOrganizeAndWriteContent How to organize and write content
          %h3#PageOrganization Page organization
          %p We encourage authors to structure pages by sections and subsections as indicated in the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Template+page"}> Template page
              \.
          %p
            Every page starts out tiny and may not seem to need a structure by subsections. But it will grow and eventually the information it provides may be hard to
            %em recognize
            without some global structure guiding the reader’s attention. After all, the natural evolution of articles makes the
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#DefinitionTheoremProofEnvironments Definition-, Theorem-, Proof environments
          %p You should include actual mathematics in appropriate environments, as familiar from textbooks and research articles in mathematics.
          %h4#LatexSyntax LaTeX-style syntax
          %p As of mid-2018, one can use environments exactly as in LaTeX.
          %p For example the code
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mo \
                              %mtext begin
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mtext theorem
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mo \
                              %mtext label
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mtext SomeTheorem
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mtext Some theorem.
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mo \
                              %mtext end
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mtext theorem
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathrlap{
                  \\backslash\text{begin}\{\text{theorem}\}
                  }
                  \\\
                  \&amp;
                  \\;\;
                  \\mathrlap{\backslash\text{label}\{\text{SomeTheorem}\}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;
                  \\;\;
                  \\mathrlap{\text{Some theorem.}}
                  \\\
                  \\mathrlap{\backslash\text{end}\{\text{theorem}\}}
                  }
          %p produces:
          %blockquote
            %p
            #SomeTheorem.num_theorem
              %h6 Theorem
              %p Some theorem.
          %p The available environments are listed below. Some environments can be specified in several ways.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Environment
                %th Syntax
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Definition
                %td definition
              %tr
                %td defn
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Theorem
                %td theorem
              %tr
                %td thm
              %tr
                %td Proof
                %td proof
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "3"} Proposition
                %td proposition
              %tr
                %td prop
              %tr
                %td prpn
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Remark
                %td remark
              %tr
                %td rmk
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Corollary
                %td corollary
              %tr
                %td cor
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Lemma
                %td lemma
              %tr
                %td lem
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Notation
                %td notation
              %tr
                %td notn
              %tr
                %td Terminology
                %td terminology
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Conjecture
                %td conjecture
              %tr
                %td conj
              %tr
                %td Scholium
                %td scholium
              %tr
                %td{:rowspan => "2"} Assumption
                %td assumption
              %tr
                %td assum
              %tr
                %td Example
                %td example
              %tr
                %td Exercise
                %td exercise
              %tr
                %td Statement
                %td statement
          %p
            Labels for the purpose of referencing work exactly as in LaTeX: put
            %code \label{SomeTheorem}
            anywhere inside the environment block. Later, one can reference it as Theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SomeTheorem"}
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/source/HowTo"} source
            of this page for how to produce this reference.
          %p Any character but closing brace is allowed in labels here (note that this is different from equation labels below).
          %h4#older_syntax Older syntax
          %p
            An older syntax for this is described at
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/wiki/instiki/show/Theorems"} Instiki theorem environments
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definition"} definition
            should be enclosed in
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_defn}
                 ###### Definition
                
                  (Definition goes here)
                
                 =--          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            should be enclosed in
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_prop}
                 ###### Proposition
                
                  (Proposition goes here)
                
                 =--          %p or
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_theorem}
                 ###### Theorem
                
                  (Theorem goes here)
                
                 =--          %p
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            in
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .proof}
                 ###### Proof
                
                
                   (Proof goes here.)
                
                 =--          %p A corollary in
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_cor}
                 ###### Corollary
                
                  (Corollary goes here)
                
                 =--          %p and a remark in
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_remark}
                 ###### Remark
                
                  (Remark goes here)
                
                 =--          %p Beware:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Contrary to the behaviour with the LaTeX syntax described above, you do need to use the labels exactly as given above. That is, it needs to be
                = succeed "," do
                  %code defn
                = succeed "," do
                  %code thm
                = succeed ";" do
                  %code cor
                %code definition
                nor
                %code theorem
                nor
                %code corollary
                (nor other abbreviations such as
                = succeed ")" do
                  %code def
            %li#WhitespaceAfterMarukuEnvironment
              %p
                There
                %em must
                be a whitespace after the closing
                = succeed "." do
                  %code =--
                %em all
                following code (unless and until it happens to spot an unpaired
                %code =--
                further down the line).
          %p
            Hence best to instead use the above
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LatexSyntax"} LaTeX syntax
          %h3#CrossLinks Cross-links
          %p
            A central point of the
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %em linked
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Therefore you should hyperlink the central keywords in what you write, by enclosing them in square brackets. Ideally, each and every keyword would be hyperlinked, certainly when it occurs first. Don’t assume that the reader has the same background as you have. If you explain something by invoking other technical terms, at least provide links to
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Analogously, when you create a new page, make sure that it is
            %em linked to
            from at least one relevant other page. An entry on a new kind of
            %em manifold
            deserves to be linked to from a list of “related entries” at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
          %p
            Apart from guiding users to your entry this way, notably this will also help search engines to index the
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#MathematicalConventions Mathematical conventions
          %p
            There is really no such thing as a “global convention on the
            = succeed "Lab\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p Therefore the general rule is: whatever you write in some entry, try to make sure that you provide enough context that the relevant conventions are clear. If you find yourself using some notation for the first time on a page and are worrying if readers will read it as intended, be sure to add a parenthetical remark (where we use here the convention that…) or similar.
          %h2#special_typesetting_features Special Typesetting Features
          %h3#how_to_typeset_mathematical_formulas How to typeset mathematical formulas
          %p
            The nLab uses the
            %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/~distler/blog/itex2MML.html"} itex2MML
            software with custom in-house hacks to typeset formulas. Its syntax is based on TeX (supporting many macros from plain TeX and LaTeX), with some important unfortunate differences documented below. The reasons for these differences are mostly historical: when the nLab was created in 2008, there was no standardized software to support formulas (like MathJax these days), with multiple competing solutions like itex2MML, jsMath, and others.
          %p
            One of the notable ways in which iTeX differs from LaTeX is that in iTeX’s math mode, a string of letters without spaces in between is interpreted as a single identifier. This has the advantage that you can invent new identifiers without needing to use
            %code \operatorname
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %code \DeclareMathOperator
            %em don’t
            want a string of letters interpreted that way you need to put spaces in between. For example,
            %code $sin(x)$
            produces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin(x)
            which is probably what you want, but
            %code $h=gf$
            produces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi gf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h=gf
            %code $h=g f$
            to get
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h=g f
            = succeed "," do
              %code $\sin(x)$
          %p iTeX is not LaTeX. Their similarity can make the differences all the more jarring. Here is a list of the differences that we know about together with suggested work-arounds.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Numbers are one thing. The syntax
                %code $10^10$
                produces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 10
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^10
                in iTeX but
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 1
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{1}0
                in LaTeX. The
                %strong safe
                syntax is to always use braces:
                %code $10^{10}$
                is consistent.
            %li
              %p
                Numbers include punctuation. Periods and commas
                %em within
                numbers are numbers. This is so that
                %code $1,000,000.00$
                (or
                %code $1.000.000,00$
                if you are European) renders correctly as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1,000,000.00
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1,000,000.00
                and not
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 000
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 000
                        %mo .
                        %mn 00
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {1},{000},{000}.{00}
                %code $t^3.2$
                renders as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi t
                        %mn 3.2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^3.2
                instead of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi t
                          %mn 3
                        %mn .2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^{3}.2
                %code $t^3,2$
                renders as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi t
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^3,2
                like you'd expect from TeX, so the two differences don't interact entirely logically. This might have been different in earlier versions of iTeX.)
            %li
              %p
                Operator names. Since iTeX does not have macro support, extending the list of operator names would be difficult except for the fact that neighbouring strings of letters get combined into an operator name. Thus
                %code $cos$
                produces
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi cos
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cos
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mi o
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c o s
                = succeed "," do
                  %code \cos
                %code $\cos$
                renders correctly in both cases. Where the operator name is not a standard LaTeX one, say
                = succeed "," do
                  %code Poly
                %strong safe
                syntax is to use
                = succeed ";" do
                  %code \operatorname
                %code $\operatorname{Poly}$
                renders correctly as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Poly
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Poly}
                in both situations. If you
                %em don't
                want an operator name, then use spaces; thus
                %code $c o s$
                produces
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mi o
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c o s
            %li
              %p
                Whitespace in embedded text. In LaTeX
                %code $x \text{ and } y$
                renders as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mtext and
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\;\text{and}\;y
                but in iTeX it renders as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mtext and
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\text{ and }y
                %code mtext
                elements should be swallowed. The
                %strong safe
                syntax is
                = succeed "." do
                  %code $x\;\text{and}\;y$
            %li
              %p
                Two-character relations. In LaTeX, two neighbouring relation symbols are combined into one relation symbol. Thus
                %code $y := f(x)$
                becomes
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:lspace => "thickmathspace", :rspace => "thickmathspace"} :=
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \mathrel{:=} f(x)
                %code $y = -f(x)$
                becomes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = -f(x)
                because a minus sign is a unary operator instead of a binary relation. Since MathML doesn't know the difference between unary operators and binary relations, it is inconvenient for iTeX to do this, so
                %code $y := f(x)$
                comes out as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y := f(x)
                instead. The
                %strong safe
                syntax is
                = succeed "," do
                  %code $y \mathrel{:=} f(x)$
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:lspace => "thickmathspace", :rspace => "thickmathspace"} :=
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \mathrel{:=} f(x)
                %code $y \coloneqq f(x)$
                produces
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≔
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \coloneqq f(x)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/~distler/blog/itex2MMLcommands.html"} available
            %li
              %p
                Non-ascii characters. iTeX is designed only to parse the mathematical sections of any page and so it assumes that any strange characters will be generated via iTeX commands such as
                %code \infty
                instead of being directly input as ?. iTeX actually takes an extreme view on this and will not parse mathematics if it includes non-ascii characters. In ordinary mathematics this should not be a problem since one should use the iTeX commands themselves to produce such characters. Where it can be a problem is in
                %code \text
                commands embedded in mathematics because these are
                %strong still
                parsed by iTeX even though it does nothing with them. Thus
                %code \text{?}
                is not a legal construct and will simply produce
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mtext ?
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{?}
                %code \text{$\infty$}
                will not work. There are two possibilities:
              %ol
                %li
                  Break the
                  %code \text
                  command:
                  %code \text{in~}\infty{-categories}
                  produces
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mtext in
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mn ∞
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi categories
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{in}\;\infty{-categories}
                  (note the forced space, simple whitespace there will not work).
                %li
                  Use
                  %strong numbered
                  entities:
                  %code \text{in ∞-categories
                  produces
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mtext in ∞-categories
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{in ∞-categories}
          %h4#how_do_i_make_lessthan_and_greaterthan_signs How do I make less-than and greater-than signs?
          %p
            The characters &lt; and &gt; are interpreted as beginning or ending HTML tags. To make a less-than or greater-than sign in iTeX math mode, use
            %code \lt
            and
            %code \gt
            respectively. For example,
            %code $0\lt 1$
            produces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\lt 1
          %h3#how_do_i_get_accented_characters How do I get accented characters?
          %p
            You can use HTML/XML/SGML
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_XML_and_HTML_character_entity_references"} character entity references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://unicode.org/charts/charindex.html"} Unicode Character Names Index
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby
            has a
            %a{:href => "http://tobybartels.name/characters/"} complete list of HTML and XML character entity references
            (or try the Unicode Databank pages listed below) with individual pages where you can check browser compliance (originally for when you couldn't take even things like &amp;harr; for granted, but even now &amp;lrm; and &amp;thinsp; may be lacking). While you can use this technique for mathematical symbols too, it's best to do those in iTeX as explained above.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/"} Unicode Data Bank
              pages:
              %ol
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/sm.html"} Math Symbols
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/mathematical-operators.html"} Math Operators
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/miscellaneous-mathematical-symbols-a.html"} Math Miscellaneous A
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/miscellaneous-mathematical-symbols-b.html"} Math Miscellaneous B
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/letterlike-symbols.html"} Letterlike Symbols
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/miscellaneous-symbols.html"} Miscellaneous Symbols
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/general-punctuation.html"} General Punctuation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/ps.html"} Open Punctuation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/po.html"} Other Punctuation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/pe.html"} Close Punctuation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/geometric-shapes.html"} Geometric Shapes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/so.html"} Other Symbols
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sql-und-xml.de/unicode-database/dingbats.html"} Dingbats
          %h3#HowToMakeLinksToSubsections How to make links to subsections of a page
          %h4#links_to_sections Links to sections
          %p When you create a section header, you can add an HTML anchor tag to it with the following syntax:
          %pre
            %code ## Heading {#anchorname}
          %p
            %strong Note that the tag must be placed at the end.
            Then you can make a link to it using the syntax:
          %pre
            %code [[some page#anchorname|link text]]
          %p If the link is to the same page then the page name can be omitted by using the syntax:
          %pre
            %code [link text](#anchorname)
          %p
            %strong Note that a single bracket link with only an anchor doesn’t function correctly when viewing a particular revision or a difference between revisions.
          %p#strange Strangely single bracket link texts allow math expressions while double bracket texts don’t. For example
          %pre
            %code [$Set^\to$](Sierpinski+topos) vs [[Sierpinski topos|$Set^\to$]] vs [$Set^\to$](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos).
          %p produces
          .standout
            %p
              %a{:href => "Sierpinski+topos"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              vs
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              vs
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              \.
          %p
            Of course, you can link to it from outside the nLab by adding
            %code http://ncatlab.org
            at the beginning of the link.
          %p
            Note that if you skip the first step, it is still
            %em possible
            to create a link to a subsection, since (at least if the page has a table of contents) every section on the page is automatically assigned an HTML anchor by Instiki. However, using such links is
            %em not
            encouraged, since the automatically generated anchor names will change whenever the page is rearranged and go away if a manual anchor name is added, which will cause such links to break.
          %p#thispgh
            The same type of syntax enables one to link to arbitrary text blocks such as paragraphs or list elements, such as a
            %a{:href => "#thispgh"} link
            to this paragraph itself.
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Note that these tags must be at the start of the text
          %pre
            %code * {#xyz} Reference
          %p which can then be linked to from within the same page by writing
          %pre
            %code [link text](#xyz).
          %h4#definitionpropositiontheoremnumbering Definition/Proposition/Theorem-numbering
          %p When you write a numbered definition or proposition or theorem, you can also simultaneously create an anchor.
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LatexSyntax"} LaTeX-style syntax
            %code \label
            inside the theorem/etc. environment as usual.
          %p In the older syntax, write:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 +-- {: .num_defn #definitionname}
                 ###### Definition
                 ...
                 =--
                
                
                 +-- {: .num_prop #propname}
                 ###### Proposition
                 ...
                 =--
                
                
                 +-- {: .num_theorem #theoremname}
                 ###### Theorem
                 ...
                 =--          %p Then you can link to it in the same ways:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 [[some page#theoremname|see this theorem]]
                 [see this theorem](#theoremname)          %p
            When you link to a theorem on the
            %em same
            page, however, it’s better to use the
            %code \ref
            syntax (without the space between
            %code \ref
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %code {
          %pre
            %code see Theorem \ref {theoremname}
          %p This inserts the number as well as creating a hyperlink, and will also work properly when the page is exported to LaTeX.
          %h4#equation_numbering Equation numbering
          %p To make an equation be automatically numbered use angular brackets instead of dollar signs
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 \[
                   \exp(\pi i) + 1 = 0 
                 \]          %p To refer to this numbered equation, add a label
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 \[
                   \label{SomeEquation}
                   \exp(\pi i) + 1 = 0 
                 \]          %p
            and then refer to it later in the text by using
            %code \eqref
            just like in LaTeX:
          %pre
            %code See equation \eqref {SomeEquation}.
          %p
            (Leave out the space after
            = succeed "," do
              %code \eqref
          %p
            There is also an older, non-TeX syntax for equation references (use an actual colon
            %code :
            in place of
            = succeed "," do
              %code -colon-
          %pre
            %code See equation (eq-colon-SomeEquation).
          %p#LabelNames
            Only word characters are allowed in LaTeX display labels. In particular, the hyphen character
            %code -
            cannot be used. This is in contrast to labels for theorem environments. This is due to use of different parsers. Instead of \label{foo-bar}, consider \label{FooBar}.
          %h4#table_of_contents Table of contents
          %p
            Since 2019, the recommended way to make a table of contents for a page (and almost any page should have a table of contents) is to write the following at the top of the page, or wherever the table of contents is to appear, without the space between the backslash and
            = succeed "." do
              %code tableofcontents
          %pre
            %code \ tableofcontents
          %p An older method is to write
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                     * table of contents 
                
                
                     {: toc}
          %p (including the line break!) at the position where the table of contents is to appear. You may want to label the table of contents by writing
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 # Contents #
                 * table of contents 
                 {: toc}          %p or (at the top of the page)
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 # [page title] #
                 * table of contents 
                 {: toc}          %p (which also gives the opportunity to use a more nicely formatted page title if special characters or mathematical formatting are wanted).
          %p The items in the table of contents will be the section headlines marked by
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                 ## top level headline ##
                
                
                 ### second level headline ###
                
                
                 etc.          %p Instead of “table of contents” after the asterisk, you can write anything you like: this line will not be displayed but is still required to kick in the CSS that makes this possible.
          %p It is important that the section headings not contain anything that shouldn’t go in the table of contents. Whilst formatting is allowed, wiki-links are not (since then the entry in the table of contents would have a link inside of a link).
          %h3#HowToCiteAndRecordReferences How to cite and record references
          %p
            At the end of each
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %pre
            %code ## References
          %p or sometimes
          %pre
            %code ## Literature
          %p which contains a bullet list of relevant references. A standard way to format an entry is this list is the following:
          %pre
            %code * {#LastnameAnotherlastnameYear} [[Firstname Lastname]], [[Anotherfirstname Anotherlastname]], _Title_, Journal **volume** issue (year) firstpage-lastpage &amp;lbrack;[arXiv:xxxx.yyyyy](https://arxiv.org/abs/xxxx.yyyyy), [doi:xyz](http://doi.xyz)&amp;rbrack;
          %p which comes out as
          %blockquote
            %ul
              %li#LastnameAnotherlastnameYear
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HowTo"}> Firstname Lastname
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HowTo"} Anotherfirstname Anotherlastname
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Title
                %strong volume
                issue (year) firstpage-lastpage &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/xxxx.yyyyy"}> arXiv:xxxx.yyyyy
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://doi.xyz"} doi:xyz
          %p Given such an item in the list of references, a standard way to reference it from the main text of the entry is as follows:
          %pre
            %code see ([Lastname &amp; Anotherlastname Year, theorem 1.3](#LastnameAnotherlastnameYear))
          %p which comes out at
          %blockquote
            %p
              see (
              %a{:href => "#LastnameAnotherlastnameYear"}> Lastname &amp; Anotherlastname Year, theorem 1.3
              )
          %p This produces a hyperlink, and in fact such that following it brings up the above bullet item highlighted by a gray box.
          %h3#query How to make comments and ask questions
          %p
            In general, the place to make comments and ask questions is at the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussions/?CategoryID=0"} nForum
          %p
            Whilst questions are answered and discussions are carried out at the nForum, the
            %em conclusion
            should often be recorded on an nLab page. If it’s about a particular page, then of course put it on that page; if it’s a general question then it could be recorded on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HowTo"} this How To
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                FAQ
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/FAQ"} ?
          %p
            That said, the wiki software does contain a mechanism for putting questions and comments on a wiki page itself: edit the page and put your comment or question in a
            %strong query block
            as shown in this example:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                +-- {: .query}
                How do I ask a question?
                =--          %p which produces
          .query
            %p How do I ask a question?
          %p
            In general, this is the wrong way to go about asking a question. When you put a query box on a page, often none of the other contributors will notice it for a long time, so it will take a long time for the question to be answered, and in the meantime it makes the page cluttered and ugly for other readers. However,
            %em occasionally
            it may be appropriate to record a brief question in a query box, if it has already been discussed at the nForum and the conclusion was not satisfactory. In this case a link to the nForum discussion should be included in the query box in preference to any lengthy detail about the question and discussion.
          %p (Before the nForum existed, it was much more common to ask questions using query boxes. Some such query boxes still remain undiscovered on various pages, but whenever we find one we generally migrate it to the n-Forum.)
          %h3#standout How to make a standout box
          %p
            If you want to make some text stand out (an important theorem, or slogan), you can do it using a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong standout box
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                +-- {: .standout}
                First quantization is a mystery, but second quantization is a functor. 
                =--          %p which produces
          .standout
            %p First quantization is a mystery, but second quantization is a functor.
          %h3#how_to_include_one_page_within_another How to include one page within another
          %p
            If you have some material at a page called
            %code foo
            that you want to include directly in pages called
            %code bar
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %code baz
          %nowiki
            %code [[!include foo]]
          %p
            in
            %code bar
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %code baz
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contents"} contents
            is included at the tope of this page. Also see how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contents"} contents
            itself has been formatted so that it will appear as a sidebar when included.
          %p
            Besides such sidebars that appear in many pages, you can also use inclusion to put in something that contains a bunch of ugly code (such as raw
            = succeed "," do
              %abbr{:title => "scalable vector graphics"} SVG
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#IncludeSVG"} here
            %code bar/foo
            and then put
          %nowiki
            %code [[!include bar/foo]]
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %code bar
            %code bar
            itself, which is why
            %code bar
            appears in the name of the included page.
          %p
            Note that the included page goes directly in where it is called with no surrounding whitespace. This can mean that formatting rules are broken on the include. For example, if the included file starts and ends with a
            %code div
            tag and is included with no surrounding blank lines then this breaks the rules and will generate an error.
          %h3#HowToUploadFiles How to upload files
          %p To upload a file, proceed as follows:
          %ol
            %li
              %p Type
              %p
                %code [FileName](/nlab/files/filename.xyz)
              %p into the edit page for some entry;
            %li
              %p hit “submit”;
            %li
              %p in the rendered entry, find a grayish link labeled “FileName”;
            %li
              %p click on the link to open an upload dialogue and follow the instructions there;
              %p notice that the dialogue field “Description” is asking for the text that will appear hyperlinked to your file. If you put no text here, no link to your file will appear by itself;
            %li
              %p complete the dialogue by hitting “upload”.
          %p After this, the file is now sitting at this URL:
          %pre
            %code https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FileName.xyz
          %p Alternatively,
          %ol
            %li
              %p Type
              %p
                %code [[FileName.xyz:file]]
              %p into the edit page for some entry, or
              %p
                %code [[FileName.xyz:pic]]
              %p in the case of a picture;
            %li
              %p hit “submit”;
            %li
              %p in the rendered entry, find a grayish link labeled “FileName.xyz” followed by a question mark;
            %li
              %p click on the question mark to open an upload dialogue and follow the instructions there;
              %p notice that the dialogue field “Description” is asking for the text that will appear hyperlinked to your file. If you put no text here, no link to your file will appear by itself;
            %li
              %p complete the dialogue by hitting “upload”;
            %li
              %p find in the rendered entry the previously grayish text “FileName.xyz” replaced by the text that you entered into the “Description”-box in the file upload dialogue, and hyperlinked to your file;
            %li
              %p due to a bug, if you want that link to persist, you need to make any further edit to the entry (e.g. add a whitespace) and “submit” again.
          %p After this, the file is now sitting at this URL:
          %pre
            %code https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FileName.xyz
          %p Hints:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                If you uploaded a picture using the
                %code :pic
                syntax and do not wish to tweak the displayed image in any way, there is no need to do anything. If you uploaded it using the
                %code :file
                syntax, you will probably want to
                %em remove
                the file upload code
              %pre
                %code [[FileName.xyz:file]]
              %p (which renders to a link to the file) and instead add a line like
              %pre
                %code &lt;img src="/nlab/files/FileName.xyz"/&gt;
              %p
                which makes the actual image show up in entry, There are a number of other posible parameters for an
                %code imagefromfile
                block, see .
            %li
              %p If you uploaded a pdf (or similar) for a reference, you will probably want to make sure that the “Description” text in the upload dialogue is “pdf” (or similar), so that the code
              %pre
                %code A. Name, _A title_, A Journal, AYear (&lt;a class='existingWikiWord' href='/nlab/files/FileName.xyz' title='HoTT proposal 2009'&gt;HoTT proposal 2009&lt;/a&gt;)
              %p renders to
              %p
                “A. Name,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A title
              %p with “pdf” hyperlinked to your file.
            %li
              %p
                There is a size limit for files to upload. The functionality is meant to be used for uploading
                %em ingredients
                of
                = succeed "Lab" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %p
                If you try to abuse the file upload for archiving your personal files, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/steering+committee"} steering committee
                will intervene.
          %h3#how_to_use_redirects How to use redirects
          %h4#example_plural_nouns_and_capitalization Example: Plural nouns and capitalization
          %p To redirect the plural
          %pre
            %code [[categories]]
          %p to the existing page
          %pre
            %code [[category]]
          %p
            simply edit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            and insert
          %pre
            %code [[!redirects categories]]
          %p
            at the bottom of the page. From then on, whenever you link to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %pre
            %code [[category|categories]]
          %p
            For example, if you open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            for editing and look at the bottom, you will find
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                [[!redirects categories]]
                [[!redirects Category]]
                [[!redirects Categories]]          %p
            Therefore,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Category"} Category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories"} Categories
            all redirect to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            now.
          %h4#caveat Caveat
          %p
            Redirects only work if the desired redirected page
            %code [[categories]]
            = succeed "." do
              %em does not already exist
          %p Don’t worry, this is a feature, not a bug, designed to avoid silly things like infinite redirect loops, etc.
          %p
            In the event that
            %code [[categories]]
            does exist already, then it probably should not if
            %code [[category]]
            already exists.
          %p
            If you run into this situation, you can help by transferring material from
            %code [[existing pages]]
            to
            %code [[existing page]]
            and change the name of
            %code [[existing pages]]
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %code [[existing pages &gt; history]]
            %em and
            we maintain the history of any revisions that were made to
            = succeed "." do
              %code [[existing pages]]
          %h4#example_links_with_nonascii_symbols Example: Links with non-ASCII symbols
          %p
            Currently, there are many pages with titles that would ideally include symbols, e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            Prior to redirects, if you wanted to link to this page, you could either write the
            %em ugly
            version
          %pre
            %code [[(infinity,1)-category]]
          %p or worse, if you were using the plural version you could write
          %pre
            %code [[(infinity,1)-category|(infinity,1)-categories]]
          %p Alternatively, you could get a little fancy with
          %pre
            %code $(\infty,1)$-[[(infinity,1)-category|(infinity,1)-categories]]
          %p which preserves the distinction between math and text but doesn’t allow the link to cover the entire linking phrase.
          %p
            Currently, if you open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            for editing and look at the bottom, you will see
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                [[!redirects (∞,1)-category]]
                [[!redirects (∞,1)-categories]]
                [[!redirects (infinity,1)-categories]]          %p
            This means that we can now simply insert links like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            and be redirected to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            %code $2$-[[2-category|category]]
            for this reason.)
          %h4#caveat_2 Caveat
          %p
            The
            %code ∞
            symbol included in the link is not obtained via the itex syntax
            = succeed "." do
              %code $\infty$
            %em or
            simply find the ‘∞’ symbol already displayed somewhere, copy it, and paste the symbol into the edit box. To see what I mean, you can edit this page to look at the source code for the link:
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
          %h4#undoing_a_redirect Undoing a Redirect
          %p
            Occasionally you may come across a situation where a redirect exists for a link that you’d like to create a separate entry for. For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructivism"} constructivism
            is currently redirected to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and remove the line (likely at the bottom of the edit box)
          %pre
            %code [[!redirects constructivism]]
          %p
            Now you can create
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructivism"} constructivism
            as usual and any pages that had previously been redirected to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            will now point to your new entry.
          %hr/
          %p
            See also
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/instiki/show/Syntax#redirecting"} the official Instiki guidelines
            on redirects.
          %h3#ParenthesisInHyperlinks How to handle parentheses in hyperlink URLs
          %p Since the Instiki code for hyperlinks
          %pre
            %code [link text](url)
          %p
            uses parenthesis, the parser apparently gets confused when the
            %code url
            itself contains parenthesis. To work around this one can
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                “escape” the parenthesis by replacing in the
                %code url
              %p
                “
                %code> (...)
                ” by “
                %code> %28...%29
                ”
          %p or
          %ul
            %li
              %p fall back to the HTML tag
              %p
                %code &lt;a href="url"&gt;link text&lt;/a&gt;
          %p or
          %ul
            %li#ReferenceStyleLinks
              %p use reference-style links
              %p
                %code [link text][id]
                %code [id]: url
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Of course, you need to replace “id” here by a
                  %em distinct
                  alphanumeric string for every distinct url on a given page (which also means that this syntax is error-prone, especially when copy-and-pasting material).
          %p For example
          %pre
            %code [Monad (category theory)#Monads and adjunctions](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monad_%28category_theory%29#Monads_and_adjunctions)
          %dl
            %dt produces
            %dd
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monad_%28category_theory%29#Monads_and_adjunctions"} Monad (category theory)#Monads and adjunctions
          %p or
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                [Monad (category theory)#Monads and adjunctions][MonadURL]
                
                [MonadURL]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monad_(category_theory)#Monads_and_adjunctions          %dl
            %dt produces again
            %dd
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monad_(category_theory)#Monads_and_adjunctions"} Monad (category theory)#Monads and adjunctions
          %h3#ToAddAFloatingTOC How to add a floating table of contents
          %p
            Many pages include a “floating table of contents” at the top right-hand side with links to other pages on similar topics. The lists of related pages are separate pages with names such as
            = succeed ";" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory+-+contents"} category theory - contents
          %p To add a floating TOC to a new page, in such a way that it will be automatically collapsed until moused over, use code such as the following at the top of the source code:
          %pre
            %code
              %nowiki
                +-- {: .rightHandSide}
                +-- {: .toc .clickDown tabindex="0"}
                \### Context
                \#### Category Theory
                +-- {: .hide}
                [[!include category theory - contents]]
                \=--
                \=--
                \=--
          %p
            If you want to include multiple contents pages, you can repeat the four lines from the one starting with
            %code ####
            through the first
            %code =--
            line.
          %p
            You can also copy-and-paste a version from existing sources, such as at
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/source/topos"} /nlab/source/topos
          %h3#diagrams How to draw commutative diagrams and pictures
          %h4#TikZ TikZ
          %p
            You can enter basic
            = succeed "-diagrams" do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PGF/TikZ"}
                %code TikZ
          %p \begin{tikzpicture}
          %p …
          %p \end{tikzpicture}
          %p or
          %p \begin{tikzcd}
          %p …
          %p \end{tikzcd}
          %p
            and adding usual
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code TikZ
          %p#QuiverTool
            A graphical user interface for generating TikZcd code is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+%28editor%29"} quiver editor
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://q.uiver.app/"} q.uiver.app
            %code LaTeX
            at the bottom to copy-and-paste the code to be inserted into the above environments.)
          %p#TikZSupportIsAHack
            Beware that this functionality is a hack: The
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code tikz
          %p
            In practice this means that
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code TikZ
            = succeed "-code," do
              %code instiki
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %code instiki
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code TikZ
          %blockquote
            %p
              (There used to be a claim here that
              %code \usetikzlibrary
              can be called inside the
              = succeed "-code" do
                %code tikz
