          %h4#xymatrix Xymatrix
          %p As of 2019 you can format commutative diagrams in the nLab using xymatrix command, almost as you would in a LaTeX document. The only significant differences are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Use a
                %code \begin{xymatrix} ... \end{xymatrix}
                block rather than a
                %code \xymatrix{...}
                block.
            %li
              %p
                Some global parameters can be passed inside the
                %code \begin{xymatrix}
                tag. For example, as usual, one can use
                %code @=1.5em
                to increase row and column separation, replacing
                %code @
                by
                %code @C
                (resp.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %code @R
                = succeed "." do
                  %code \begin{xymatrix@=1.5em}
                %code \begin{xymatrix[font = \large, border = 2em]}
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %code \begin{xymatrix@=1.5em[font = \large, border = 2em]}
                %code font
                controls the font size throughout the diagram, and
                %code border
                adds some whitespace around the figure, which is sometimes needed when using curved arrows. One can use only one of the two parameters instead of both.
          %p Under the hood, this works by running pdflatex and then generating an SVG from the resulting pdf, which is included in the HTML source of the rendered page.
          %p Use Tikz rather than Xymatrix if you do not have a strong preference or have not used either before. But it is fine to use Xymatrix if this is what you are used to.
          %h5#examples_2 Examples
