          %h3#how_to_edit_nlab_pages_in_your_favorite_text_editor How to edit nLab pages in your favorite text editor
          %p (Firefox - and clones - specific)
          %p
            One way to do this is to install one of several firefox extensions, e.g.
            %a{:href => "https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/edit-with-emacs1/?src=search"} for Emacs
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/ghosttext/?src=search"} for Sublime Text, Atom, VS Code, and Vim
            %a{:href => "https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/find-replace-for-text-editing/?src=search"} Find &amp; Replace for Text Editing
            allows you to use regular expressions and text templates.
          %p
            If your favorite editor is
            %a{:href => "http://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/"} Emacs
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.gnu.org/software/auctex/"} AucTeX
            %code .emacs
            file:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                (add-to-list 'auto-mode-alist '("/\\(www.\\)?ncatlab.org" . latex-mode))
                (add-to-list 'auto-mode-alist '("/golem.ph.utexas.edu" . latex-mode))
                (defun nlab-latex-fixes ()
                  (when (or (string-match "/\\(www.\\)?ncatlab.org" buffer-file-name)
                            (string-match "/golem.ph.utexas.edu" buffer-file-name))
                  (longlines-mode t)
                  (set (make-local-variable 'TeX-open-quote) "\"")
                  (set (make-local-variable 'TeX-close-quote) "\"")))
                (add-hook 'LaTeX-mode-hook 'nlab-latex-fixes)          %p This will tell Emacs to automatically edit nLab pages (and nCafe comments as well, for good measure) in LaTeX mode, with long lines wrapped using soft returns, and ordinary double-quotes rather than LaTeX ones.
          %h3#how_to_print_a_page_from_the_nlab How to print a page from the nLab
          %p Use the ‘Print’ link at the bottom of the page. As a shortcut in most browsers on most operating systems, hit Alt-Shift-P while the focus is in the page to go to the printable page.
          %h3#how_to_customize_the_nlab How to customize the nLab
          %p (Firefox - and clones - specific)
          %p
            You may wish to customize the font scheme (both for math or text) on the nLab, as well as tweak things such as the small edit box for comments. Try using the Stylish add-on if you are using Firefox; Stylish is a plug-in for firefox enabling you to customize websites; it is available
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/2108"} here
          %p Currently, the following stylish themes are available:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://userstyles.org/styles/17934"} nLab Stylish theme
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"} Bruce Bartlett
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://userstyles.org/styles/22800"} nLab – nCafe style
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Sch%C3%A4ppi"} Daniel Schäppi
          %h3#download How to download a local copy of the nLab
          %p
            A current git repository of the source code for nLab pages is available at
            %a{:href => "https://github.com/ncatlab/nlab-content"} https://github.com/ncatlab/nlab-content
            and a current git repository of the compiled HTML code for nLab pages is available at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/ncatlab/nlab-content-html"} https://github.com/ncatlab/nlab-content-html
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/meta"} meta
